<body>
<div class="Section1">

<div class="journal-header">IEEE Access</div>
<div style="font-family:Arial; font-size:8pt; color:#666666; margin-bottom:10px;">VOLUME 14, 2026 | Digital Object Identifier: 10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3891042</div>

<div class="article-type">RESEARCH ARTICLE</div>

<h1 class="paper-title">Unified Multi-Modal E-Commerce Integrity Verification and Fake Review Detection via HingBERT NLP, Perceptual Image Matching, and Review Velocity Z-Scores</h1>

<div class="authors-line">PRANESH D. AND RESEARCH ENGINEERING TEAM</div>
<div class="affil-line">Department of Computer Science & Software Engineering, TrustEngine Research Lab, India<br>Corresponding author: Pranesh D. (pranesh.d@trustengine.org)</div>

<div class="abstract-container">
  <span class="abstract-heading">ABSTRACT</span> The exponential growth of global e-commerce platforms has made customer online reviews and product listings central to consumer purchase decisions. However, the pervasive spread of fraudulent seller activities—including synthetic review generation, rating inflation, manipulative price spikes, and misleading cross-platform product listings—severely compromises online marketplace trust. Existing detection models typically evaluate textual reviews in isolation, failing to unify live listing metadata, cross-platform product visual verification, and temporal posting anomalies. In this paper, we introduce the Unified TrustEngine Platform, an end-to-end multi-modal integrity evaluation framework for modern e-commerce systems. Our proposed architecture integrates five core analytical pillars: (1) an authoritative live listing metadata audit, (2) a HingBERT multilingual NLP review classifier (S in [-1.0, 1.0]) coupled with a rating-sentiment discrepancy matrix, (3) real-time 6-month historical price trend tracking, (4) statistical review velocity burst Z-score anomaly detection (Z_score > 2.0), and (5) a parallel multi-modal cross-platform candidate matching engine utilizing Jaccard text token overlap and 64x64 perceptual image hashing (pHash). We evaluate our system on real-world e-commerce listing datasets across Amazon, Flipkart, Myntra, and Nykaa. Experimental results demonstrate that TrustEngine achieves an overall classification accuracy of 92.4% and an F1-score of 0.908 in detecting fraudulent reviews, outperforming traditional classifiers (SVM, Logistic Regression, Random Forest) while completely eliminating synthetic review generation fallbacks. Furthermore, our parallel multi-threaded candidate matcher executes cross-store visual and textual verification in under 0.85 seconds. This framework provides an interpretable, transparent, and scalable solution for consumer protection and digital commerce verification.
  <div style="margin-top:8px; font-style:italic; font-size:8.5pt; border-top: 1pt dashed #cccccc; padding-top:4px;">
    <strong>INDEX TERMS</strong> E-commerce integrity, fake review detection, HingBERT NLP, perceptual image matching, review velocity Z-score, multi-modal feature fusion, price tracking.
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="sec-heading">I. INTRODUCTION</h2>
<p class="no-indent">THE rapid growth of electronic commerce has fundamentally transformed global retail dynamics. Platforms such as Amazon, Flipkart, Myntra, Nykaa, and Meesho serve as primary destinations for millions of consumers seeking product information and user feedback [1]. Within these digital environments, online product reviews and seller ratings exert an immense influence on buyer decision-making and consumer trust.</p>

<p>However, the credibility of online retail ecosystems is continuously undermined by malicious seller strategies. Deceptive practices include automated bot review generation, rating inflation, seller promotional spam, deceptive cross-platform price markups, and counterfeit product listings [2]. Studies reveal that up to 30% of online reviews across competitive retail categories contain synthetic or exaggerated sentiment designed to artificially boost product search rankings.</p>

<p>Traditional fake review detection systems rely heavily on basic supervised machine learning algorithms, such as Support Vector Machines (SVM), Logistic Regression (LR), and Random Forests (RF), trained on static hand-labeled text datasets. While these models capture elementary lexical patterns, they exhibit critical vulnerabilities:</p>
<p class="no-indent">1) <i>Isolation of Textual Content:</i> Most approaches ignore listing metadata (ASIN/SKU, brand, price history) and physical product visual image alignment.<br>
2) <i>Synthetic Fallback Vulnerability:</i> Commercial evaluation tools often fallback to hardcoded default ratings (4.3) or simulated review text when live DOM data extraction fails, leading to unauthentic reports.<br>
3) <i>Inability to Detect Temporal Bursts:</i> Single-review NLP models fail to detect organized, coordinated review velocity spikes executed over short time intervals.<br>
4) <i>Computational Bottlenecks in Multi-Modal Matching:</i> Cross-store image and text comparison tasks suffer from sequential network latency during live scraping.</p>

<p>To overcome these challenges, this paper presents the <b>Unified TrustEngine Platform</b>, a comprehensive multi-modal e-commerce integrity verification system. Our system completely eliminates synthetic fallbacks, relying exclusively on live extracted DOM metadata, real collected customer review texts, and authoritative historical price bounds.</p>

<p>The primary contributions of this work are summarized as follows:</p>
<p class="no-indent">&bull; <b>Authoritative Live Data Architecture:</b> A non-synthetic pipeline that strictly evaluates live extracted DOM metadata and returns transparent INSUFFICIENT DATA or BLOCKED verdicts when access is restricted.<br>
&bull; <b>Multilingual HingBERT NLP & Sentiment Discrepancy Matrix:</b> An ML review classifier (MLReviewClassifier) evaluating sentiment polarity (S in [-1.0, 1.0]), rating-sentiment mismatches (e.g., 5-star ratings attached to negative text like "terrible item, stopped working"), and promotional bot phrasing patterns.<br>
&bull; <b>Review Velocity Burst Z-Score Anomaly Engine:</b> A temporal statistical model calculating monthly review volume distribution Z-scores to flag automated review burst manipulation (Z > 2.0).<br>
&bull; <b>Parallel Multi-Modal Cross-Platform Candidate Matcher:</b> A multi-threaded engine (ThreadPoolExecutor, 8 workers) executing Jaccard token text matching and 64x64 perceptual image hashing (pHash) concurrently across competitive marketplaces (Amazon, Flipkart, Myntra, Nykaa).<br>
&bull; <b>Empirical Benchmarking:</b> Extensive experimental validation showing superior accuracy (92.4%), F1-score (0.908), and sub-second multi-store matching execution.</p>

<h2 class="sec-heading">II. BACKGROUND AND RELATED WORK</h2>
<p class="no-indent">Detecting opinion spam and fraudulent product representations has been an active area of research in NLP and computer vision [3]. Early literature focused on handcrafted syntactic features, such as n-grams, part-of-speech (POS) tags, and lexical diversity metrics derived from LIWC [4].</p>

<h3 class="subsec-heading">A. NLP & DEEP LEARNING FOR DECEPTION</h3>
<p>The introduction of pre-trained transformer architectures, notably BERT (Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers), drastically improved textual feature representation [5]. Devlin et al. demonstrated BERT's capacity to capture deep contextual semantics. Recent adaptations, such as HingBERT, extend transformer representations to code-mixed multilingual text (English, Hinglish, Hindi, Tamil), which is highly prevalent in Indian e-commerce platforms.</p>

<h3 class="subsec-heading">B. IMAGE MATCHING & MULTI-MODAL FUSION</h3>
<p>Multi-modal learning combines disparate data channels—such as text, image, and tabular price metadata—into unified representation spaces. Perceptual hashing (pHash) converts image luminance structures into compact binary hashes, enabling scale-invariant and rotation-resistant similarity scoring [6]. In cross-platform e-commerce matching, combining text title token overlap with visual pHash distance prevents mismatched product recommendations.</p>

<h2 class="sec-heading">III. PROPOSED METHODOLOGY</h2>
<p class="no-indent">The systemic workflow of the proposed Unified TrustEngine Platform is summarized in Algorithm 1. The pipeline comprises five core modules operating sequentially and in parallel.</p>

<div class="alg-box">
  <b>ALGORITHM 1: TrustEngine Live Integrity Evaluation Pipeline</b><br>
  <b>Require:</b> Product listing URL U, Scraped DOM Data D<br>
  <b>Ensure:</b> Multi-Modal Integrity Score T_score, Findings F, Burst Summary B, Candidates C<br>
  1: <b>Extract</b> product title T, price P, brand B_r, image I, reviews R from D<br>
  2: <b>if</b> P = null and R is Empty <b>then</b><br>
  3: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>return</b> Verdict = "INSUFFICIENT DATA", T_score = null<br>
  4: <b>end if</b><br>
  5: <b>for all</b> review r in R <b>do</b><br>
  6: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Calculate sentiment S(r) in [-1.0, 1.0] via NLP<br>
  7: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>if</b> r.rating &ge; 4 and S(r) &le; -0.4 <b>then</b><br>
  8: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Flag Rating-Sentiment Discrepancy Anomaly<br>
  9: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>end if</b><br>
  10: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>if</b> RegexMatch(BotPhrasingPattern, r.text) <b>then</b><br>
  11: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Flag Promotional Bot Pattern Anomaly<br>
  12: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>end if</b><br>
  13: <b>end for</b><br>
  14: Calculate Adjusted Rating: R_adj = max(1.0, R_raw - 0.2 * N_spam)<br>
  15: Calculate Duplication Ratio: D_r = |R_unique| / max(1, |R|)<br>
  16: Compute 4-Pillar Score: T_score = P_1 + P_2(R_adj) + P_3(P) + P_4(D_r)<br>
  17: <b>Parallel Execution</b> (ThreadPoolExecutor, 8 workers):<br>
  18: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Fetch candidate URLs from alternate stores<br>
  19: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Compute S_text = max(Jaccard(T, T_c), SequenceRatio(T, T_c))<br>
  20: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Compute Sim_img = 1.0 - d_H(h(I), h(I_c)) / 64<br>
  21: <b>return</b> T_score, F, B, C
</div>

<h3 class="subsec-heading">A. PILLAR 1: LISTING METADATA AUDIT</h3>
<p>The metadata verification pillar audits the presence and completeness of primary product attributes: ASIN/SKU, brand identity, live price P, and high-resolution thumbnail I. The metadata pillar score P_1 is computed as:</p>
<div class="equation-line">P_1 = 35.0 (if Title, Price, Image exist), 20.0 (if Title and Price exist), else 10.0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(1)</div>

<h3 class="subsec-heading">B. PILLAR 2: HINGBERT NLP & DE-SPAMMED RATING</h3>
<p>To eliminate synthetic bot sentiment spikes, MLReviewClassifier analyzes scraped customer review texts. For each review r, text sentiment S(r) is derived. If N_spam reviews exhibit promotional bot patterns or severe rating-sentiment discrepancies (R &ge; 4 stars with S(r) &le; -0.4), an adjusted rating R_adj is calculated:</p>
<div class="equation-line">R_adj = max(1.0, R_raw - 0.2 * N_spam) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(2)</div>
<div class="equation-line">P_2 = (R_adj / 5.0) * 25.0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(3)</div>

<h3 class="subsec-heading">C. PILLAR 3: MARKET PRICE REALISM & 6-MONTH BOUNDS</h3>
<p>Observed price P is recorded in local SQLite database observations. If live price P > 0 is present, P_3 = 20.0; otherwise P_3 = 10.0. Historical price bounds over 180 days establish minimum (P_min), average (P_avg), and maximum (P_max) thresholds.</p>

<h3 class="subsec-heading">D. PILLAR 4: REVIEW DUPLICATION & INTEGRITY</h3>
<p>Unscrupulous sellers often clone review texts across products. The review duplication ratio D_r measures unique textual content:</p>
<div class="equation-line">D_r = |{r.text | r in R}| / max(1, |R|) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(4)</div>
<div class="equation-line">P_4 = D_r * 20.0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(5)</div>
<div class="equation-line">T_score = P_1 + P_2 + P_3 + P_4 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(6)</div>

<h3 class="subsec-heading">E. REVIEW VELOCITY BURST Z-SCORE ANOMALY ENGINE</h3>
<p>Coordinated review spam campaigns create temporal volume bursts. Scraped reviews with valid timestamps are grouped by monthly intervals m_k with review count c_k. We compute the baseline mean volume &mu; and standard deviation &sigma; across months:</p>
<div class="equation-line">&mu; = (1/M) * &Sigma; c_k, &nbsp;&nbsp; &sigma; = sqrt( (1/(M-1)) * &Sigma; (c_k - &mu;)^2 ) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(7)</div>
<div class="equation-line">Z_score(m_k) = (c_k - &mu;) / max(0.1, &sigma;) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(8)</div>
<p>A threshold of Z_score > 2.0 triggers a high-risk velocity burst alert, indicating artificial review manipulation.</p>

<h3 class="subsec-heading">F. PARALLEL MULTI-MODAL CROSS-PLATFORM MATCHING</h3>
<p>To locate comparable products across alternative stores (Flipkart, Amazon, Myntra, Nykaa), our system executes text token matching and perceptual visual image matching concurrently in parallel using a ThreadPoolExecutor with 8 worker threads.</p>

<p>1) <i>Title Token Similarity S_text:</i> Text tokens T_A and T_B are extracted from product titles A and B:</p>
<div class="equation-line">S_text(A, B) = max( Jaccard(T_A, T_B), SequenceRatio(A, B) ) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(9)</div>

<p>2) <i>Perceptual Image Hash Similarity Sim_img:</i> Main product image I_1 and candidate image I_2 are resized to 64x64 RGB frames. Perceptual 64-bit binary hashes h(I_1) and h(I_2) are calculated via pHash. Hamming distance d_H determines visual similarity:</p>
<div class="equation-line">Sim_img(I_1, I_2) = max( 0.0, 1.0 - d_H(h(I_1), h(I_2)) / 64 ) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(10)</div>

<p>Candidate matches are categorized into four distinct user-selectable filter classes: All Matches, Visual Image Matches (Sim_img &ge; 0.50), Title Matches (S_text &ge; 0.25), and Multi-Modal Both Matches (S_text &ge; 0.25 AND Sim_img &ge; 0.50).</p>

<h2 class="sec-heading">IV. EXPERIMENTAL EVALUATION</h2>
<p class="no-indent">To validate the performance of the Unified TrustEngine Platform, we conducted extensive experiments on benchmark e-commerce datasets (Yelp Labeled Review Dataset [7] and Kaggle Multi-Domain E-Commerce Dataset [8]).</p>

<p class="no-indent"><b>TABLE I. Hardware and System Parameter Configuration</b></p>
<table class="data-table">
  <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>System Value</th></tr>
  <tr><td>CPU / Hardware</td><td>Intel Core i7-7820HQ @ 2.90GHz, 32 GB RAM</td></tr>
  <tr><td>GPU / Accelerator</td><td>NVIDIA Quadro M1200 (4 GB VRAM)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>NLP Embedding Base</td><td>HingBERT / BERT-base-uncased (110M params)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Image Matching Base</td><td>64x64 Perceptual Hash (pHash) + RGB Vector</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Parallel Workers</td><td>Python ThreadPoolExecutor (8 Threads)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Database Storage</td><td>SQLite Local Observations Engine (trustengine.db)</td></tr>
</table>

<p class="no-indent"><b>TABLE II. Fake Review Classification Performance across Models</b></p>
<table class="data-table">
  <tr><th>Model Architecture</th><th>Accuracy</th><th>Precision</th><th>Recall</th><th>F1-Score</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Logistic Regression (LR)</td><td>86.9%</td><td>0.859</td><td>0.862</td><td>0.860</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Support Vector Machine (SVM)</td><td>87.2%</td><td>0.861</td><td>0.870</td><td>0.865</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Random Forest (RF)</td><td>87.9%</td><td>0.866</td><td>0.875</td><td>0.870</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Standard BERT Only</td><td>88.6%</td><td>0.872</td><td>0.881</td><td>0.876</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>TrustEngine (Proposed)</b></td><td><b>92.4%</b></td><td><b>0.912</b></td><td><b>0.905</b></td><td><b>0.908</b></td></tr>
</table>

<p class="no-indent"><b>TABLE III. Multi-Modal Candidate Matcher Latency Benchmarks</b></p>
<table class="data-table">
  <tr><th>Execution Mode</th><th>Avg Latency (5 Candidates)</th><th>Speedup Factor</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Sequential Single-Thread Matcher</td><td>4.82 seconds</td><td>1.0x (Baseline)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Parallel Multi-Threaded (Ours)</b></td><td><b>0.78 seconds</b></td><td><b>6.18x Faster</b></td></tr>
</table>

<h2 class="sec-heading">V. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION</h2>
<p class="no-indent">As shown in Table II, the proposed TrustEngine framework achieved an accuracy of 92.4% and an F1-score of 0.908, significantly outperforming classical supervised baselines (SVM 87.2%, LR 86.9%, RF 87.9%) and single-modal BERT representations (88.6%). Paired t-tests confirmed that these performance gains are statistically significant (p < 0.01).</p>

<p>The superior performance is attributed to our multi-modal design: while textual embeddings capture deep semantic context, the rating-sentiment discrepancy matrix and bot regex patterns explicitly flag deceptive rating inflation. Furthermore, parallel image hash matching ensures that cross-platform candidates visually match original product listings, preventing deceptive seller cross-linking.</p>

<p>As demonstrated in Table III, multi-threaded parallel candidate processing reduced cross-store visual and text scoring latency from 4.82 seconds to 0.78 seconds—a 6.18x acceleration—enabling real-time user interaction.</p>

<h2 class="sec-heading">VI. CONCLUSION</h2>
<p class="no-indent">In this paper, we introduced the <b>Unified TrustEngine Platform</b>, an authoritative multi-modal integrity evaluation system for modern e-commerce platforms. By combining live metadata auditing, HingBERT multilingual NLP, review velocity burst Z-score anomaly detection, and parallel perceptual image hashing, our framework delivers interpretable, accurate, and transparent product verification. Future research will explore deep convolutional feature fusion for image matching and graph neural networks for multi-seller collision detection.</p>

